 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
--   **Boundary Checks:** Neighbors outside the chunk must be handled. If the chunk is part of a larger world, the algorithm must query the global world data or adjacent chunks [S3, unofficial]. If treated as a standalone chunk, a boundary policy (empty or solid) must be chosen [S3, unofficial].
--   **Vertex Duplication:** This method does not share vertices between adjacent quads. A single internal edge between two exposed faces will result in 8 vertices (4 per quad) instead of 4 [S3, unofficial].
--   **Precision:** Using integer coordinates for vertices is standard for voxel meshes, but floating-point positions may be needed for smoothing variants like Naive Surface Net [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Boundary Checks:** If the algorithm is applied to a single chunk without neighbor data, it must decide how to handle faces at the chunk boundary. The pure naive method emits them regardless [S5, unofficial]. If culling is added, boundary neighbors must be defined (e.g., "Air" or "Solid" policy) [S2, S4, unofficial].
+*   **Vertex Sharing:** The pure naive method does not share vertices, leading to high memory usage. Some implementations labeled "Naive" do share vertices but still emit all faces, which is inefficient [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** The algorithm generates $6 \times N_{solid}$ quads. For a solid $32^3$ chunk, this results in 196,608 quads [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Naive Surface Net](3679-naive-surface-net.md)
-A surface reconstruction technique that estimates vertex positions by averaging the midpoints of voxel edges crossing the implicit surface boundary, producing a smoothed mesh compared to standard Marching Cubes or simple voxel extraction. It generates geometry for all six faces of every solid voxel in a chunk, ignoring neighbors and performing no culling, building on [Naive Voxel Meshing](2376-naive-voxel-meshing.md).
+A variant that averages intersection points on voxel edges to create smoothed feature points before constructing quads, producing a smoother surface than standard voxel extraction.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
--   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of voxels in the chunk ($S^3$) [S3, unofficial]. Each voxel is processed once, and 6 faces are checked [S1, unofficial].
--   **Memory Complexity:** $O(F)$, where $F$ is the number of exposed faces [S3, unofficial].
--   **Measured Performance:** On an Apple M2 Pro (macOS 26.5.1, Node v22.22.2, three@0.185.1), naive meshing median time is 5.60 ms (range 5.12–6.30 ms, 21% spread) on a quiet machine for a 32³ terrain chunk with 17,191 solid voxels [S1, unofficial].
--   **Mesh Size:** On a solid 32³ cube, it produces 196,608 quads [S1, unofficial]. On a 32³ terrain chunk with 17,191 solid voxels, it produces 103,146 quads, 412,584 vertices, and 206,292 triangles [S1, unofficial].
--   **Strengths:** Extremely simple to implement; deterministic output; no complex data structures required [S1, unofficial].
--   **Limitations:** High vertex and index count due to lack of vertex sharing and face culling; poor performance for large solid volumes [S1, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
--   **When to use:** Prototyping, educational purposes, or as a baseline for comparison with more advanced meshing algorithms like Greedy Meshing [S1, unofficial].
--   **When not to use:** Production environments requiring efficient rendering of large voxel worlds [S1, unofficial].
+
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N^3)$, where $N$ is the chunk dimension. The algorithm iterates through every voxel and performs constant work per voxel [S2, S5, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N^3)$ for the output mesh. The number of vertices and indices is proportional to the number of solid voxels times 6 [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** On an Apple M2 Pro (macOS 26.5.1, Node v22.22.2, three@0.185.1), naive meshing a 32³ terrain chunk with 17,191 solid voxels took a median of 5.60 ms (range 5.12–6.30 ms) [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Mesh Statistics:**
+    *   Solid $32^3$ cube: 196,608 quads [S2, unofficial].
+    *   32³ terrain chunk (17,191 solid voxels): 103,146 quads, 412,584 vertices, 206,292 triangles [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Extremely simple to implement; no neighbor lookups or complex data structures required; deterministic output [S1, S5, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** High polygon count due to lack of face culling; high draw call count if not batched; inefficient memory usage due to non-shared vertices [S1, S4, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** Prototyping, educational purposes, or as a baseline for testing other meshing algorithms [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** Production environments with large worlds or performance constraints, where culling or greedy meshing is required [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Naive Voxel Meshing serves as a foundational technique in voxel terrain generation, often acting as the baseline for more advanced surface extraction methods [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. It is implemented in various voxel game prototypes and educational resources to demonstrate the conversion of voxel data to mesh geometry [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. The method provides the direct geometric input for smoothing algorithms like Naive Surface Net [S3, unofficial].
+
+Naive Voxel Meshing is a foundational technique in voxel rendering, often cited as the first step in converting voxel data into GPU-renderable geometry [S1, unofficial]. It is described in various educational resources and open-source projects as a starting point for voxel engines [S1, S4, unofficial]. The method is contrasted with more advanced techniques like Culled Meshing and Greedy Meshing, which reduce polygon counts by omitting internal faces or merging adjacent faces [S1, unofficial]. The term "Naive" highlights the lack of optimization in face generation [S4, S5, unofficial].
 
